+## Proposed security incident collector deployment — review pending
+
+This is an **unpublished security review proposal**, not a claim of deployed or accepted production behaviour. [Tracking issue #2138](https://github.com/RovxBot/homelab-infra/issues/2138) builds on merged [Stage A #2203](https://github.com/RovxBot/homelab-infra/pull/2203) and [Stage B #2204](https://github.com/RovxBot/homelab-infra/pull/2204). Deployment review: https://github.com/RovxBot/homelab-infra/pull/2205. [Image proof](https://github.com/RovxBot/homelab-infra/blob/feat/security-collector-production/ops/security-collector/production-image.json) and [qualification evidence](https://github.com/RovxBot/homelab-infra/blob/feat/security-collector-production/ops/security-collector/production-qualification.json) identify the proposed inputs and which checks remain unrun. Publish this section only after its security review; mark deployment/acceptance complete only after independently verified rollout.
+
+The existing `kubescape` Flux Kustomization will own a single collector Deployment, ServiceAccount, namespace Roles, ClusterIP health service and dedicated 256 MiB Longhorn state PVC. The process runs as UID 10001 with a read-only root filesystem, RuntimeDefault seccomp, all capabilities dropped and no host access. Requests/limits are 100m/500m CPU and 128Mi/512Mi memory; these are ceilings awaiting deployed measurement. Its projected API token uses the API server's default audience, rotates and is reread for every request; the client verifies the mounted CA/hostname, allows only GET to the configured HTTPS API origin and refuses proxies/redirects. It does not use the scanner's `kubescape.io` service token. [Kubernetes token projection](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-service-account/#serviceaccount-token-volume-projection) explains the API audience default.
+
+The 30-second loop reads only the reviewed named `metal6` node-agent logs, verifies its Pod/DaemonSet UID, ServiceAccount, pinned image and container/restart identity, and independently checks enrolled Deployment/ReplicaSet/Pod/image ownership. The initial collector scope is `homepage/homepage/homepage` and `homepage/homepage-assets/nginx`, with logical cluster identity `home`; it does not expand node-agent coverage. At the retained read observation, both enrolled workloads were outside `metal6`, so agent coverage was **0/2**. The retained R1000 refers to a deleted standalone test Pod. It is stored as an unknown unattributed source observation, **not an invented Deployment incident**. No new attack, scan or agent change was made.
+
+Authenticated reads persist normalized findings, replay receipts, cursor anchors, reader health and inventory epochs in private SQLite. Ordinary logs and empty windows never become incidents. Queues/cursors commit together and replay survives restart. Bounds include 1,000 pending observations / 8 MiB, 1,000 unattributed source receipts, 200 incident keys, 20,000 seven-day replay receipts and a 64 MiB database page limit. There is no raw telemetry retention or automatic eviction. The volume also accommodates the DELETE rollback journal and private snapshots; readiness requires at least 128 MiB free. Initial boundaries, missing overlap, agent changes, disconnection, invalid input, unavailable attribution and saturation remain explicit. Gaps latch across restart; a quiet successful read is not proof of complete detector emission.
+
+**Assessment coverage is unavailable in this rollout.** The trusted assessment reader remains available in code but is explicitly disabled by `assessment_history_available=false`; private status records `history_unavailable`. The scanner history PVC is RWO. Co-location permits a same-node mount, but a permanent second consumer could retain the attachment and block scanner relocation. This PR does not mount that volume or change its storage, CronJobs, locks, schedules or scanner placement. A separately reviewed read-only history access/failover design and exact report API privileges are needed before enabling it. Current coverage cannot prove old workload generation; only ordinary scans after an independent UID/generation/digest observation can qualify. No historical successful generation evidence is invented.
+
+Public incident writing and all automatic incident closure are disabled. The process has no GitHub client/credential and does not instantiate delivery. Qualified findings generate allowlisted private previews only. Detailed status and previews are mode-0600 `/state/private/status.json` and `previews.json`, inside a mode-0700 directory; SQLite also stores `service_status`. Unattributed source observations have no incident preview. Do not paste private output or findings into a public issue.
+
+Existing Gatus gets two checks with **empty GitHub alerts**: `cluster/security-collector-process` checks `/readyz`; `cluster/security-collector-sources` checks `/healthz`. Process readiness requires a completed loop and writable bounded storage. Source health additionally exposes aggregate read/qualification timestamps, coverage, unavailable assessment history, pending count, saturation, errors and unresolved gaps. The source endpoint remains degraded for unavailable assessment coverage or continuity gaps even when the process works. Five minutes without loop progress expires readiness/liveness and clears current-success flags; a stopped Pod also removes the service endpoint, so Gatus can detect it independently. HTTP serves no previews, detailed status or database. There is no public ingress, event receiver or new monitoring stack. Cilium enforcement stays `never`; no NetworkPolicy isolation or infrastructure egress restriction is claimed.
+
+### Release and acceptance procedure (not yet run in production)
+
+1. Require independent review of the exact PR head and green Repo Checks, Kubescape Assessment Checks and **Security Collector Image** jobs. The image job rebuilds the deterministic source layer on a pinned Python amd64 base, checks every source hash and the exact deployment digest, tests the image with non-root/read-only restrictions, publishes that same artifact and verifies anonymous digest retrieval. New GHCR packages default private: if the last step fails for package visibility, the operator must set `rovxbot/security-collector` public and rerun the job. Do not merge until the digest can actually be pulled. No automatic merge, deployment-pin update or rollout action is performed by the job.
+2. The operator merges the deployment PR. Use the established context for cooked-k8s and inspect `kubectl -n flux-system get kustomization kubescape` for the reviewed revision/Ready condition. Then inspect `kubectl -n kubescape get deployment security-collector`, the selected Pod, its imageID, securityContext, token/CA projection, probes/resources and PVC. Confirm one ready Pod, UID 10001, read-only root, no escalation/host access, state PVC Bound and three healthy Longhorn replicas. Record evidence privately; do not claim a proposed resource ceiling is a measurement.
+3. Confirm the actual projected token is working through advancing `last_runtime_read`, the verified private agent identity and saved cursor/status. Check denied reads using the **collector's token inside its own Pod**, without printing it or reading a denied response body. For example:
+
+   ```sh
+   kubectl -n kubescape exec -i deployment/security-collector -- python3 -B - <<'PYCODE'
+   from urllib.error import HTTPError
+   from urllib.request import Request
+   from security_collector.sources import KubernetesAPI
+   api = KubernetesAPI('https://kubernetes.default.svc', '/var/run/collector-api/ca.crt', '/var/run/collector-api/token')
+   assert api.get('/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/kubescape/daemonsets/node-agent')['metadata']['name'] == 'node-agent'
+   token = api.token_file.read_text().strip()
+   for path in ('/api/v1/namespaces/kubescape/secrets/collector-denied-probe',
+                '/api/v1/namespaces/kubescape/pods',
+                '/api/v1/namespaces/kubescape/pods/collector-denied-probe/log',
+                '/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/kubescape/deployments/kubevuln',
+                '/apis/spdx.softwarecomposition.kubescape.io/v1beta1/namespaces/homepage/vulnerabilitymanifestsummaries'):
+       try:
+           with api.opener.open(Request(api.origin + path, headers={'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + token}), timeout=10) as response:
+               code = response.status
+       except HTTPError as error:
+           code = error.code
+           error.close()
+       assert code == 403, 'denied-read gate failed'
+   print('verified: authenticated named read and five denied reads')
+   PYCODE
+   ```
+
+   From an operator identity permitted to impersonate the ServiceAccount, `kubectl auth can-i --as=system:serviceaccount:kubescape:security-collector` must return **no** for secrets, workload updates/deletes, jobs/token creation and exec/attach; use authorization introspection rather than trying a mutating request. Verify only the named agent log plus scoped Homepage inventory reads are permitted. The operator's exec permission is separate from the collector's RBAC.
+4. Privately inspect `/state/private/status.json`, `previews.json`, and SQLite using a `mode=ro` connection. Confirm `service_status`, `reader_status`, `cursors`, `source_observations` and any qualified incident/receipt rows advance as appropriate. Run `PRAGMA quick_check`. Verify source Pod/owner/container/image identity against current Kubernetes state. Normal or empty windows should leave incidents unchanged; the existing R1000 may remain unattributed after the deleted test Pod. Do not replace missing ownership with guessed attribution or create a synthetic attack just to obtain a preview. The focused tests qualify the renderer; a live preview requires genuinely attributable evidence.
+5. Confirm the database `writes`, `delivery` and `api_calls` tables remain empty, writer flags are false, no GitHub credential is mounted, Gatus collector endpoints have no alerts and the repository's relevant issue activity has no collector incident writes. Assessment history remains unavailable, last qualified times null unless qualified older state exists, and no scan is launched. Verify both Gatus endpoints independently; source degradation at these known gates is expected, not acceptance of full coverage.
+6. Record private cursor/receipt/incident/source-observation counts and state-PVC UID. Through an operator-reviewed GitOps template annotation change, perform an ordinary Recreate rollout, then repeat the reads. The same PVC, cursors, receipts, occurrence counts and gap state must survive; replay must not add incidents or occurrences. Leave assessments disabled and public delivery off. To qualify stopped-process detection, use an explicitly reviewed Flux scale-to-zero/restore change and observe Gatus failure/recovery; do not patch/kill the agent or change scanner scheduling.
+7. Measure actual collector CPU/memory with existing Metrics Server and inspect restart counts, filesystem/DB/queue use and three-replica Longhorn health. Confirm no storage saturation, then retain the reviewed commit, image digest, timestamps and private acceptance evidence. Publish accepted Wiki facts only after independent live acceptance; until then mark these gates **not run**. Full assessment/whole-node workload coverage remains pending even if runtime transport acceptance passes.
+
+### Rollback and recovery
+
+Stop collection through a reviewed Flux change (replicas zero or removal of the `collector` resource entry), retaining public-writer/closure disablement. Remove its Gatus endpoint configuration alongside a final removal, then remove only collector Roles/ServiceAccount/Service/Deployment. `security-collector-state` is annotated `kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled`, so reverting the deployment preserves it; verify it remains Bound. Do not delete a PVC, clear cursors/gaps/receipts, restart the scanner, remove history/reports/SBOMs or change security/network policy. No scanner volume is attached to the collector in this rollout.
+
+After the writer stops, preserve the private database and any recovery journal together before copying. On restoration reuse the retained state/PVC and reviewed image/config, check integrity/size, then resume with writes/closure disabled. SQLite locking rejects concurrent writers and Recreate avoids overlap. Runtime replay remains idempotent; any pending assessment would need fresh source qualification before draining. Historical gaps, saturation/archival and source UID changes require reviewed handling rather than automatic resets. A failed initial release has no accepted predecessor image; rollback removes the collector while preserving evidence, and Gatus must show the stop during the supervised rollback test.
